Scott 1972, §2. An element x is way below an element y when some Scott-open neighbourhood of y is contained in the principal upset of x. The infix \(x \ll y\) is notation for this relation.

`WayBelow.self_iff_scottOpen_Ici` is Proposition 2.2(vi): an element is way below itself if and only if its principal upset is Scott-open. If the element is way below itself, a witnessing Scott-open neighbourhood contained in the principal upset equals that upset, because the neighbourhood is an upper set and contains the element. Conversely, a Scott-open principal upset is a neighbourhood of the element contained in itself.

`WayBelow.sSup_iff` is Proposition 2.2(vii): for a nonempty directed set, an element is way below the supremum if and only if it is way below some member. A witnessing Scott-open neighbourhood of the supremum meets the directed set, and the meeting point inherits that neighbourhood. Conversely, the member lies below the supremum, and the same neighbourhood witnesses the relation there.

Scott 1972, Proposition 2.2(i). The bottom element is way below every element.

The universe is a Scott-open neighbourhood of the element, and every element lies above bottom, so the universe is contained in the principal upset of bottom.

Scott 1972, Proposition 2.2(ii). If x and y are both way below z, then their join is way below z.

The intersection of the two witnessing Scott-open neighbourhoods of z is Scott-open and contains z. A point of the intersection lies above both x and y, hence above their join.

Scott 1972, Proposition 2.2(iii). If x is way below y and y lies below z, then x is way below z.

The same Scott-open neighbourhood of y contains z, because a Scott-open set is an upper set, and it remains contained in the principal upset of x.

Scott 1972, Proposition 2.2(iv). If x lies below y and y is way below z, then x is way below z.

A Scott-open neighbourhood of z contained in the principal upset of y is contained in the principal upset of x, because a point above y is above x.

Scott 1972, Proposition 2.2(v). If x is way below y, then x lies below y.

The witnessing Scott-open neighbourhood of y is contained in the principal upset of x, so y lies in that upset.
